<<<
+ say ‘ the e.’ or ‘ the n.’, or * the r., qualities are courage & intelligence only’.
===
+ say ' the e.' or ' the n.', or ' the r., qualities are courage & intelligence only '.
>>>

<<<
They have reached the mecting-point, however, from different directions, bringing each its native equipment, of varying suitability for various tasks.
===
They have reached the meeting-point, however, from different directions, bringing each its native equipment, of varying suitability for various tasks.
>>>

<<<
For instance, in We can hardly say that capital is as r. to production as land & labour the least suitable of the three has been chosen,
===
For instance, in *We can hardly say that capital is as r. to production as land & labour* the least suitable of the three has been chosen,
>>>

<<<
If we call something e. we have in mind a whole that would not be what it is to be or is or was if the part in question were wanting; the e. thing is such that the other thing is inconceivable without it.
===
If we call something *e.* we have in mind a whole that would not be what it is to be or is or was if the part in question were wanting; the e. thing is such that the other thing is inconceivable without it.
>>>

<<<
Æ. is the strongest word of the three.
===
*E.* is the strongest word of the three.
>>>

<<<
When we call something n., we have in mind the irresistible action of causality or logic;
===
When we call something *n.*, we have in mind the irresistible action of causality or logic;
>>>

<<<
N. doubles the parts of indispensable & inevitable.
===
N. doubles the parts of *indispensable & inevitable*.
>>>

<<<
When we call something r., we have in mind merely an end for which
===
When we call something *r.*, we have in mind merely an end for which
>>>

<<<
The fact that r. has no negative form corresponding to une. & unn. is significant of its less exclusive meaning.
===
The fact that *r.* has no negative form corresponding to *une. & unn.* is significant of its less exclusive meaning.
>>>

<<<
that chain of cause & effect is for the present *

* j.e.in 1913; 1925 has

+ established;
===
that chain of cause & effect is for the present ⁎

+ established;
>>>

<<<
-est in superlatives. See-ER & -EST.
===
**-est** in superlatives. See ^^-ER^^ & ^^-EST^^.
>>>

<<<
estate. The three estates, i.e. the Lords Spiritual, the Lords Temporal, & the Commons, is often wrongly applied to Sovereign, Lords, & Commons.
===
**estate.** *The three estates*, i. e. the Lords Spiritual, the Lords Temporal, & the Commons, is often wrongly applied to Sovereign, Lords, & Commons.
>>>

<<<
The third e. is a phrase often used for the French bourgeoisie before the Revolution; & the fourth e. is a jocular description of the newspaper press as one of the powers that have to be reckoned with in politics.
===
*The third e.* is a phrase often used for the French bourgeoisie before the Revolution; & *the fourth e.* is a jocular description of the newspaper press as one of the powers that have to be reckoned with in politics.
>>>

<<<
esteem. For success of e., see GALLICISMS.
===
**esteem.** For *success of e.*, see ^^GALLICISMS^^.
>>>

<<<
estimate, v. See -ATABLE.
===
**estimate, v.** See ^^-ATABLE^^.
>>>

<<<
estimate, estimation. The sense a judgement formed by calculation or consideration belongs to estimate & not to estimation, which means not the judgement itself, but the forming of it.
===
**estimate, estimation.** The sense a judgement formed by calculation or consideration belongs to *estimate* & not to *estimation*, which means not the judgement itself, but the forming of it.
>>>

<<<
The tendency described in LONG VARIANTS often leads writers astray, as in: Norwegians can only wish that the optimistic estimation of Mr Ponting of the British minefields at Spitzbergen will come true.
===
The tendency described in ^^LONG VARIANTS^^ often leads writers astray, as in: *Norwegians can only wish that the optimistic estimation of Mr Ponting of the British minefields at Spitzbergen will come true.*
>>>

<<<
estimation. The use of in my &c. e. as a mere substitute for in my &e. opinion where there is no question of calculating amounts or degrees, as in The thing is absurd in my e., is illiterate.
===
**estimation.** The use of *in my &c. e.* as a mere substitute for *in my &c. opinion* where there is no question of calculating amounts or degrees, as in *The thing is absurd in my e.*, is illiterate.
>>>

<<<
Tories love discussion: disproved it, we read.
===
*Tories love discussion:*

Footnote: ⁎ i.e. in 1913; 1925 has disproved it, we read.
>>>
